    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice",
    "problem": "$\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x=$",
    "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{9}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27}-\\frac{2D}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3}.$",
